WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.699 --> 00:00:15.829
I'm Mark Oppenheimer. I'm the director

00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:18.689
 of the Yale Journalism Initiative, and we're
delighted

00:00:18.689 --> 00:00:20.800
to sponsor this event along with the

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.090
Office of Sustainability of Yale University. 
Holly Parker, and Erin Sturgis-Pascale and I have--

00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:30.799
I was about to say we worked hard, but actually we haven't worked terribly hard--

00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:32.300
sufficiently hard to bring together

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.610
two people who

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.730
are remarkable presences is the

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.450
debates about

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.019
parking, traffic,

00:00:42.019 --> 00:00:42.670
sustainability,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:43.689
the environment,

00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:46.020
and so forth.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.969
Donald Shoups is going to speak first,

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:52.050
and then we're going to go right into Tom
Vanderbilt's presentation, and we'd ask that you reserve

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.480
all of your comments and questions for the
end, and they've both been incredibly gracious

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.030
in suggesting that we allow ample time for
comments and questions

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.310
at the end.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.480
As a preparatory matter, let me just say that
we

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.710
one always wonders of putting together an
event

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:07.860
at Yale College

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.550
what's the demand for this event and

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.100
how small a room would it have to be so that
it seems crowded,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.300
and 

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.560
I'm just

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.760
delighted to say that we guessed right, and
that in fact there

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:20.619
is

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:23.520
real curiosity about the questions that
they ask

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.830
and about the research

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.119
that they do,

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:27.640
and

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.320
Shoups was a graduate of Yale College and
also got his doctorate in economics here.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.409
He has since taught at the University of Michigan
in Ann Arbor

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:40.750
and also at the University of California in
Los Angeles where for some time he has served as

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.399
professor.

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:45.820
As many of you probably know, he's the author
of "The High Cost of Free Parking",

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.799
and perhaps most

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:51.399
prestigiously he has been referred
to by

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:53.900
no less an authority than the Wall Street
Journal

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.510
as a parking rock star.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.110
I think he's the only parking rock star

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.490
in the room anyway.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.530
And he will be followed by Tom Vanderbilt who
is

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.209
a graduate of the University of Wisconsin and is
a long time

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:13.569
freelance journalist for

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:16.019
publications like the New York Times Magazine,

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:16.850
Slate,

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:17.639
Wired,

00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:21.059
and even Gourmet.

00:02:21.059 --> 00:02:22.969
He is the author of "Traffic",

00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:24.229
which has won him the

00:02:24.229 --> 00:02:29.329
status of--he's been called
a traffic guru,

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:30.140
and so

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.069
along with the parking rock star we have the traffic
guru,

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.240
and between the two of them we think they're going to
present a very

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.849
exciting

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:39.749
evening of discussion of

00:02:39.749 --> 00:02:41.559
traffic, parking, and our

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:43.740
green future, so please make sure that your

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.469
mobile phones are off at least set to some sort of
very quiet

00:02:48.469 --> 00:02:49.429
setting,

00:02:49.429 --> 00:02:50.960
and please join me

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.170
in welcoming first

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:59.170
professor Donald Shoups.

00:03:12.839 --> 00:03:17.269
Well, thank you for inviting me to speak to
you tonight. It's an honor to be here speaking

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:21.789
in the classroom where I can remember being a
student.

00:03:21.789 --> 00:03:23.069
For those of you

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:27.759
who are students or can remember their days
as students, I think

00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:30.559
you may have figured out that

00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:32.959
college professors come in two types.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:36.559
I don't mean the men and the women or the liberals
and the conservatives;

00:03:36.559 --> 00:03:39.849
I mean the dogs of the cats.

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:42.710
The dogs run around in packs.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:46.029
They bark a lot, and each one tries to become the
top dog,

00:03:46.029 --> 00:03:48.359
but the cats are solitary.

00:03:48.359 --> 00:03:50.739
They like to mark territories

00:03:50.739 --> 00:03:54.499
as their own so that other cats will stay out.

00:03:54.499 --> 00:03:57.089
And among professors of transportation planning,

00:03:57.089 --> 00:03:59.799
the dogs chase after moving cars,

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:05.540
or they ride around in cars with their
heads hanging out the window, excited by speed,

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.330
but cats do not like riding around in cars,

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:12.650
and they never hang their heads out the window.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:13.979
Cats

00:04:13.979 --> 00:04:16.630
instead inspect all of the parked cars,

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:21.169
and they mark the tires.

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:26.059
They also enjoy sleeping on the hood of a newly
parked car where the engine is still warm.

00:04:28.299 --> 00:04:31.400
Cars are parked ninety five percent of the
time,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:36.150
so while dogs chase after the cars the five
percent of the time that they're moving,

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.809
I felt I could learn something about the
ninety five percent of the time that the cars

00:04:40.809 --> 00:04:42.149
are parked.

00:04:42.149 --> 00:04:47.059
Tom Vanderbilt and I divided it up. He gets
the five percent of the time the cars are

00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:47.659
moving, and

00:04:47.659 --> 00:04:52.139
I get the other ninety five percent of the
time.

00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:54.370
But I think that parking is very important

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.889
for many reasons.

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:03.889
Say, in a group this large, several of you were probably
even conceived in a parked car.

00:05:05.239 --> 00:05:09.979
I was surprised to learn that I've been called
a parking rock star, which of course is not a

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:15.449
real rock star, but still I'm thinking of
changing my name to Shoup Dog.

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:24.360
It's also an honor for somebody from California, especially
Los Angeles, to be

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.419
asked to talk about

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:27.759
transportation planning

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:30.970
in Connecticut or New England

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.099
because

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:33.480
here's the view that

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.280
most initiatives seem to have

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.849
of California.

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:40.220
And it is true that we've paved a lot over with parking lots,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.099
and the easterners think that we've

00:05:43.099 --> 00:05:45.220
even chopped down all the redwood trees

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.979
to build gas stations.

00:05:48.979 --> 00:05:51.499
There are some, but

00:05:51.499 --> 00:05:52.750
I think

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.139
like California, too much of

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:59.340
urban America looks like this picture of
silicon valley.

00:06:00.899 --> 00:06:03.300
There's a light rail line in the lower

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.529
right hand corner of this picture, but you
couldn't call it transitory and a development,

00:06:07.529 --> 00:06:12.809
and we tend to ignore this asphalt blight in
our life especially when we're parked free on it as

00:06:12.809 --> 00:06:19.099
all of these cars are at two o'clock on a weekday
afternoon.

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:22.800
But parking is free to us only in our role
as motorists

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:23.860
of course.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:28.740
Just because we don't pay for parking doesn't
mean the costs cease to exist

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:34.509
We pay for all this parking dearly in every other
aspect of our life as a homeowner as employees,

00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:36.979
as a taxpayer,

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:42.409
as a citizen.

00:06:42.409 --> 00:06:49.270
Silicon valley in California are not the only
places where parking dominates the landscape.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:52.949
Here is of a picture of New Haven

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:57.639
There's a lot of parking in that--
Let's see, I think I have two pictures of

00:06:57.639 --> 00:06:58.749
New Haven.

00:06:58.749 --> 00:07:00.350
Let's see, here's one.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:05.630
Yes, where is this? I think you can see there's
Temple Street garage in the picture, 

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:14.120
and the connector, which was built when I was a student,
which really seems to be more of a divider.

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:17.650
Here's another view of the

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.310
part of the city.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.339
There's an awful lot of parking in New Haven.

00:07:21.339 --> 00:07:24.830
If more parking were the solution to the
urban problems,

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:29.639
New Haven would be a very prosperous city.

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:31.309
There's a parking garage that was

00:07:31.309 --> 00:07:32.669
built when I was a student.

00:07:32.669 --> 00:07:35.439
It's quite a wonderful piece of sculpture,

00:07:35.439 --> 00:07:40.499
and it works very well as a garage, but I
don't think we should tell ourselves that more

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:43.129
parking is going to

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:48.029
of solve many of our problems.

00:07:48.029 --> 00:07:52.550
It was built of course as part of an urban
renewal project. Large parts of New Haven were

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:56.750
torn down to provide the space
for this, and

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:00.910
the shopping center that I can remember being
built is already been torn down right next

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.419
to this building.

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:07.040
So I'd like to start with some

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:13.149
background. I think for both Tom's and my talk.

00:08:13.149 --> 00:08:20.149
Our automobiles by themselves consume an eighth of the 
world's oil supply every day.

00:08:20.559 --> 00:08:27.559
And we import two-thirds of this oil.
And we're paying for it with borrowed money.

00:08:28.689 --> 00:08:31.419
We have more vehicles than cars.

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:32.050
And it

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:35.930
doesn't seem like it when you're a student or
a faculty member at Yale,

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:42.360
but American drivers park free for ninety
nine percent at the end of all of their trips.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.379
And I don't think this is sustainable.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.100
And the question is are we going to have a hard landing
or can we figure a way to solve some

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.430
of our problems. I think we can.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:53.660
I think we've made

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.070
two big mistakes, and one

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.690
is to keep curb parking

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.650
free or cheap

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.870
and to require lots of off-street parking.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.450
Here's the parking requirements of the
city of New Haven,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:10.350
which are quite modest compared to most
cities' parking requirements.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.959
They look very scientific. Somebody must know
how many parking spaces

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:18.240
a vocational trader business school needs.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:24.660
Or a "game room" as defined in section 42.2.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.150
I think if you

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:28.600
asked any of these planners where those numbers

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:29.690
could come from,

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:33.760
none of them could explain a single
one of these numbers.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.770
It looks scientific,

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:38.480
but it isn't.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.249
I think that the

00:09:40.249 --> 00:09:45.910
pretense to knowledge of us planners,
that we can tell you how many parking spaces

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.670
must be supplied before anything can be built

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:50.520
or any business can be opened,

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:51.800
that's done a lot of harm.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.660
I think it's

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:57.780
really a great planning disaster, which Peter Hall
defines as something that costs a lot

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:00.670
of money and has gone seriously wrong.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:04.080
I think that all of those parking skews all of our

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.820
travel choices toward the car as the natural

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:13.010
way to get where you want to go. It caused
a lot of sprawl; it spreads buildings apart

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:13.920
to provide room for

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.100
the parking between them.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:21.230
A lot of the building's design is degraded
by having to provide all the parking, and

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:26.270
that makes housing more expensive because
it has to include the cost of the parking, and

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:31.050
this is something that even
many planners don't understand. It makes

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.930
it very difficult to reuse older buildings

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:34.929
because if you want to

00:10:34.929 --> 00:10:37.240
open a

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.880
restaurant in the former shoe store,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:42.400
the planners will say, "Well, where's the required
parking?"

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.980
And if you don't have the required parking,
you can't open the restaurant.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:50.500
So older buildings can't fulfill the role
that they could

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.470
in the city.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:59.430
It limits home ownership. It's very difficult to

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:00.839
convert apartment buildings

00:11:00.839 --> 00:11:05.110
where people rent into condominiums
that they could own.

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:08.720
It damages the urban economy. I think it's
done that in New Haven.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.990
You don't have to

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.370
explain the fact that it harms the environment.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:20.060
So some cities are solving this problem
in a new way. It's to get the price of parking

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.200
right. Here's

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:26.470
some of the reforms that are going on. It's
to charge the right price for parking.

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:27.890
By that I mean

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:32.620
the lowest price you can charge and have
one or two vacant spaces on every block.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:36.180
That's about an eighty five percent occupancy
rate. So the parking is well used,

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:40.710
it's almost full, but is readily available.
Anybody driving down any street in your city

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.740
can always see one or two open spaces

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:48.250
ready to park in front of any store or restaurant
so you can go in.

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:53.250
And cities have made this politically popular
by returning the meter revenue to the neighborhoods

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.920
that generated it. If you put in parking meters,
and charge

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.720
performance prices for parking, you'll get the
revenue.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:04.070
And that has  this tended to make it very popular
with the merchants

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:08.480
and the land owners and the residents who
benefit from the added public services in

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:10.120
their neighborhoods.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:11.410
Once you do that,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:16.559
you could remove or or all produce at least
the minimum parking requirements

00:12:16.559 --> 00:12:20.679
because you won't have to, nobody can
say there's not enough parking because wherever

00:12:20.679 --> 00:12:23.220
you go you'll see plenty of parking.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:27.180
And I think this freedom from parking requirements
will mean you can use any building for any

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:30.880
business that's legal.

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:38.320
Now here's an example of what they're doing
in San Francisco. They're starting this policy,

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:43.130
and this is just an example of the prices
that they expect to charge.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:47.900
The prices will be the lowest price
they can charge in any hour and still have one or two

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.850
vacant spaces on every block. So it can
be different in different times of day,

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.060
it can be different in

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:54.550
different parts of the city,

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:57.380
it can be higher at the second hour.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:00.640
Once you say that we have a principle

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:05.540
the we understand, this is the price that we
want, the price that will lead to

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.230
convenient parking

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:12.470
and well used parking, that's how we're going
to set the price. We're not going to let

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:16.040
just inertia set prices.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:20.350
And because they have a twenty million
dollar grant from the federal government

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:25.520
to institute this. They have a lot of money for graphic artists, and

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.679
they're important for anything you want to do.

00:13:27.679 --> 00:13:32.360
Here's an example of what they
say is what's wrong with the city. That some blocks

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.100
have

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:37.810
all the spaces occupied, and other blocks

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:39.970
have some vacancies.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:45.279
So the idea is you raise the price on the
top block and you lower it on the bottom block,

00:13:45.279 --> 00:13:47.069
and you get something like this.

00:13:47.069 --> 00:13:48.930
Isn't that simple?

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:50.069
Some people

00:13:50.069 --> 00:13:55.609
think that charging these market prices for parking
are an enormous social

00:13:55.609 --> 00:13:59.780
upheaval compared-- like the reformation or
prohibition--

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:01.849
some enormous change in the way we do

00:14:01.849 --> 00:14:02.720
live our lives.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.079
But look what a small change

00:14:05.079 --> 00:14:08.680
can achieve what is a good result.

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:11.810
So it's very easy to legislate this. Here's

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:13.940
the exact

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.800
ordinance language of one city

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.410
in northern California

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:22.819
is that the city council ceased trying
to set the price for

00:14:22.819 --> 00:14:26.809
every parking space and micromanaging,
saying it should be a dollar an hour within the

00:14:26.809 --> 00:14:27.850
two hours.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:31.870
They didn't want a policy to do, what they
did is set the policy

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.540
of what they want the parking

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:35.529
supply to do,

00:14:35.529 --> 00:14:38.330
to be readily available

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:39.730
but well used.

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:43.210
They establish it. They give the

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.170
transportation authority

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:50.960
the responsibility to move
the price up and down. And the council members

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:54.640
can just look out the window and see if they've
done their job.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:59.770
If the spaces are half empty then the price
is too high.

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:02.340
If the spacebar all full, the price is too low.

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:03.840
And they made this popular

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:05.359
by

00:15:05.359 --> 00:15:07.110
returning the money to the

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.740
meter zone.

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:15.560
So that all the affected parties,
the stakeholders as we call them now, supported it.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:19.220
It passed the city council unanimously.

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:23.030
They also removed the time limits on curb parking

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:27.360
because if the prices are going to create vacancies,

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.140
you don't need to force turnover.

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:34.890
You shouldn't have a low price and force people
of spaces at the end of twenty four minutes,

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:37.780
thirty minutes, or an hour.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:41.910
You should let people park as long as
they're willing to pay because the curb park

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:46.510
is more expensive than the off street
parking, and anybody who wants to park for a long time

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:50.420
naturally gravitates to the off street parking.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:52.880
So it's as simple as the Goldilocks principle

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:58.240
of parking prices. We all learn this as
children. No bed should be too

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:03.620
soft or too firm, and parking prices shouldn't
be too high or too low.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:04.660
And I think

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:08.580
one of the problems with our existing system
of having low prices

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:12.990
is that it creates cruising for curb parking,
which is driving around the block, we've all

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:14.180
done it

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:18.140
many times hunting for a curb parking space.

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:21.240
Here's a situation you could face in

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:22.810
almost any city where the

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:25.260
curb parking is cheaper the off street parking.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:30.080
How long would you be willing to drive around
hunting for a curb space rather than pay to park

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:35.310
off street? We all face this problem

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:37.170
and because of this problem

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:38.890
there's an awful lot of 

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:41.810
cruising for curb parking. And here's
the survey,

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:44.660
a sort of summary of surveys.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:50.230
The first one is in 1927, appropriately
in Detroit,

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:50.999
finding

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.199
a fifth to a third of all the cars on the
street

00:16:56.199 --> 00:16:57.900
were cruising for curb parking.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:01.090
The most recent ones were in New York with a very
clever

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:02.889
technique

00:17:02.889 --> 00:17:04.340
of

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:08.250
interviewing drivers who were stopped at
the traffic lights and asking them if they

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.030
were hunting for curb parking.

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:11.360
And you can see here

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:15.310
spot twenty eight percent and another was
forty five percent of the cars were cruising

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:16.230
for parking.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:21.929
That is an environmental and a
transportation crime.

00:17:21.929 --> 00:17:25.080
Just think, the wasted fuel, the air pollution,

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:28.570
creating congestion,

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:32.330
and this is sort of what looks like. This was
a study done in Chicago in 1939

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:37.520
where they stationed, well I'm sure they were
undergraduates at a lot of corners,

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.910
and they noted

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:44.430
every car the past every intersection,
wrote down the license plate and the exact time

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:47.679
so they could recreate the path of travel of
every car.

00:17:47.679 --> 00:17:51.880
Some people were obsessed with parking around one block,
and some people were open to new experiences,

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:54.810
try something different.

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:55.720
And this leads to

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:57.409
a lot of very

00:17:57.409 --> 00:17:59.460
risky behavior.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:04.660
People will, they're only focused on trying
to get a parking space.

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:07.730
They ignore bicyclists, they

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:10.140
ignore pedestrians. 

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.090
I've seen it happen, a car sees across a vacancy

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:15.669
on the other side of the
street will make u-turn in the middle

00:18:15.669 --> 00:18:18.670
of the street, which is very dangerous. Here's some

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:21.050
grainy footage I got and researched

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:28.050
in Westwood.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:39.690
I was able to interview the driver
afterwards, and she says she knows it's

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:40.890
cheaper to park at

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:47.890
the curb, and she does whatever it takes to get
a curb parking space.

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:55.610
Well, here is the results that we found
in Westwood village. We studied

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:57.370
this for year,

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:02.570
and in this little fifteen block area there
were almost a million miles of vehicle

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:06.750
travel hunting for underpriced curb parking.
And after you get to this little fifteen

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:07.790
area,

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:10.870
they're a million miles a year of traffic

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:13.360
hunting for underpriced curb parking.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:18.140
We found that on average there were two cars
circling every block, and it took only three

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:22.240
minutes to find a space, but they're so
many people who park in a year,

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:24.799
it all adds up. That's equal to

00:19:24.799 --> 00:19:27.510
four trips to the moon

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:33.070
in a fifteen block area is happening all
over the Earth.

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:35.080
All over the Earth.

00:19:35.799 --> 00:19:39.690
Some people will say it's un-American
to charge

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:44.799
for curb parking even if it does,
if it would help the environment,

00:19:44.799 --> 00:19:49.010
but I think it's very American to charge people
for what they use.

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.450
Here's a picture of

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:53.520
homeowners around the LA

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:58.090
Coliseum during the Olympics in 1984,
and it happens during every event

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:00.280
at the Coliseum, that the residents

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:04.809
park their own cars on the street and rent
out their driveways to

00:20:04.809 --> 00:20:08.300
take it over.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:12.400
Some people think it's too difficult to do what
I'm saying. They have the idea that all

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:16.530
parking meters are like the original parking meters
in 1935 that

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:21.100
you put your money in the slot, and you turn the lever,

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:23.820
you hope to get back before your time runs out.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:25.880
But then when they were invented they were

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:31.890
condemned as being a a devilish combination
of an alarm clock and a slot machine.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:33.040
You take your risk.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:37.730
But that's because most Americans don't know
all the progress that's happened everywhere

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:44.030
else on Earth. We're now the lagging behind the rest
of the Earth in meter technology.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:46.350
Here's a parking meter of the sort that

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:50.920
some cities have put in. Maybe New Haven has them.
This is on the UCLA campus,

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:55.480
designed to look like a retro parking meter.

00:20:55.480 --> 00:21:00.009
It doesn't even accept coins. It accepts
credit cards and dollar bills.

00:21:00.009 --> 00:21:04.520
It doesn't tell you what the price is until you
push a button,

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:08.260
any button, and then it tells you what the price is
at that time.

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:12.080
And here it is, it doesn't even tell you what the
price per hour is Of course it's three dollars for the

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:14.870
first hour and four dollars for the second
hour.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:17.710
Well, let's see. Is it too high? Is it

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:20.630
asking too much for college students to
pay?

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:25.080
Well I say you can't tell whether the price is
right until you look at the results.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:26.970
So I think some of you are

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:29.900
aware that professors have a lot of spare
time,

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:34.590
and I set up my camera across from the

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:39.440
meter and looked at eight parking spaces
controlled by this meter. See, there's the 

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:41.480
meter and there's somebody paying.

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:46.300
And I think that one out of eight spaces should
be vacant. Around that.

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:50.790
But let's look what happened during the hour.
You can see the shadows move as the time goes by.

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:52.880
Some people leave,

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:54.590
some people don't.

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:57.809
Sometimes there were all the spaces full,
but there were quickly

00:21:57.809 --> 00:22:04.809
vacancies, sometimes two vacancies.

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.910
Once three vacancies,

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:10.710
then they filled up.

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:13.600
So this is what happened at the
end of the hour.

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:16.360
And I think that

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:21.020
this show to me that was the right price
in that location at that time.

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:26.760
And I don't see how anybody could argue it's
too high

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:28.500
or it's too low.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:31.659
What principle would you use to say that
price was wrong?

00:22:31.659 --> 00:22:34.210
It would be different at a different
time,

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:37.480
but that's what I'm arguing for.

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.559
Now I think that it's easy enough to do,

00:22:40.559 --> 00:22:44.690
that we have a choice. Our first future is just to
change nothing,

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:47.309
but I think inertia is the

00:22:47.309 --> 00:22:53.620
driver of most curb parking policies and curb
parking prices in the United States.

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:55.519
Keep curb parking free or cheap,

00:22:55.519 --> 00:22:58.810
require ample off-street parking.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:05.560
And we'll live in this world with this appalling
ad where people are told to buy a cheap car.

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:11.120
Low income people are told to buy a cheap
car rather than ride on the bus.

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:17.710
We'll buy lots of cars and big cars and park them
for free. Here's the front of the Kodak Theatre on Hollywood

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:18.690
Boulevard.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:23.670
We'll need lots of roads for these cars. Here's
in New Brunswick, Maryland.

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:25.929
We'll need lots of freeways,

00:23:25.929 --> 00:23:29.610
and we'll have plenty of congestion.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:33.860
With people on their way to their parked car, to their 
free parking.

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:36.610
But we do have a

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:40.710
possibility for a second future, which I think
is far better.

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:44.090
We could charge performance-based prices 
for curb parking.

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:46.840
We could use the revenue to

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:51.360
prepare sidewalks, to plant street trees, to put
wires underground,

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:57.600
to improve neighborhoods, to pay for public
infrastructure.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:03.500
And then we can remove off-street parking requirements.

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:06.100
Here's the kind of world,

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:08.270
some people say this is he's recommending.

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:15.400
But let me conclude right where I began in Silicon Valley.
Suppose you bought into this policy,

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:17.870
and

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:18.640
certainly

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:23.130
I'm kind of an evangelist as you can tell,

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:26.140
trying to convince people that were doing
the wrong thing, and we should do the right thing.

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:31.330
Suppose they bought my ideas, remove the parking
requirements on this land,

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:35.250
which is now committed to parking. You
couldn't build anything else on it because it wouldn't

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:36.899
have the required parking.

00:24:36.899 --> 00:24:42.030
That that building is as big as it can be
given that parking lot.

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:44.080
Suppose you say we remove the parking requirements

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:47.039
and allow them to build on this lot.

00:24:47.039 --> 00:24:51.060
Now I picked buildings from the University of London
because they look nice. I was speaking to the

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:54.620
Congress for New Urbanism, and they like these
buildings,

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:55.649
and they must have just

00:24:55.649 --> 00:24:56.620
been cleaned,

00:24:56.620 --> 00:25:02.150
but we wouldn't get that in California,
so I just took more pedestrian apartment buildings 

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:05.010
in Los Angeles. Suppose you tried it.

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:09.580
And remember everybody in Silicon Valley complains
that the traffic congestion is terrible,

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:13.880
the cost of housing is so high, the commutes are so long.

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:20.470
This would address all of those problems.
And remember, all of those cars, or many of those

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:25.140
cars in that lot are imported from California.
You cannot important apartment houses, so if

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.520
you were an electrician or a plumber

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:32.690
or a dry wall builder or a roofer, you'd
probably think this was a great idea.

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:36.640
If you were a worker at Cisco Systems, you could
walk across the parking lot

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:38.390
to get to work.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:43.860
If that worked out, if they made much more
money by using that land for housing rather

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:46.300
than for their employees,

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:47.549
job adjacent housing,

00:25:47.549 --> 00:25:49.480
than for free parking, 

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:51.740
they could just keep on doing it, and it could

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:53.840
quickly become to look like a city.

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:58.300
As you walked along that sidewalk, the curb
parking wouldn't be free,

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:04.690
but there would be street trees, and there would
be buildings, it would have ground floor retailers.

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:09.039
What would we do with all the parking garages that
we won't need, all the Temple Street garages?

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:16.370
Here's one in Holland. They found a wonderful
use for it. I think the Yale students could

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:23.370
do this too.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:27.020
I think that

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:28.990
these reforms are needed

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:33.460
unless General Motors succeeds with its ace
in the hole, has really got a

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:35.650
great idea.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:40.820
If it succeeds, I think everybody at Yale will
buy one of these cars.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:47.820
Here's the ad for it.

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.030
I think that would be

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:54.950
great for Yale students.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:57.059
And faculty and staff!

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:00.820
Well I think planners should always conclude

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:05.870
with a quote from Jane Jacobs,
that we aren't rich

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:07.720
because of what we have done.

00:27:07.720 --> 00:27:12.030
We are a prosperous nation because of what we've
inherited,

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:13.500
and it's up to us

00:27:13.500 --> 00:27:14.690
to make it

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:20.380
to continue this prosperity, and I think our
current parking policies

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:23.080
are driving us in the wrong directions,

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:24.460
and I think that

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:27.329
we have a President who likes to quote

00:27:27.329 --> 00:27:31.749
Abraham Lincoln. There's nobody better to quote in
United States' history. I think our case

00:27:31.749 --> 00:27:32.470
is new, and

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:33.760
I think that

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:35.879
maybe ten years ago it would have

00:27:35.879 --> 00:27:40.420
been unthinkable to recommend market prices
for curb parking,

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:45.039
and planners rejected makers as sort of
an alien ideology.

00:27:45.039 --> 00:27:48.300
But I think now we're more willing to look at

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:49.659
prices as the way to solve our

00:27:50.419 --> 00:27:51.870
problems.

